WEBVTT

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:06.900
So, I guess my first question is: how did you meet Dad?

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:13.650
I met your dad, Ian, when we both joined a beginners' tennis club.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:19.080
So we both started to play tennis as adults in our 20s.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:23.580
We were part of a group, and we were just friends.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.260
And then we went on holiday in a group to the south of France.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:34.000
And then we came back, we got together, and the rest is history, as they say.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.540
Yeah. Oh, wow.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.410
Yeah, obviously I know this story, but it's still interesting.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.380
Yeah. I heard that there was a little bit of competition, actually.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.220
Really? Yeah. Who with?

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:48.980
Oh, it's a secret between me and Dad, that.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:54.740
Oh, right. Okay, so where did you go on your first date?

00:00:54.740 --> 00:01:01.640
We went to a little pub in Leicestershire called The Reservoir,

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.250
which is on the edge of a reservoir in Leicester.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.750
And we had a nice drink there.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.740
And then we went on and we had

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:17.480
a curry in a curry restaurant in Leicester,

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:18.770
which is fantastic for curries.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:23.630
And it was memorable because Ian had a vindaloo.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.220
Yeah. He had a vindaloo, which is a very,

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.930
very hot curry, and about three jugs of water.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.753
So it was quite funny.

00:01:32.753 --> 00:01:35.473
So, was he trying to show off?

00:01:35.473 --> 00:01:40.133
Yes. I think he was trying to impress me with,

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:42.553
you know, how spicy a curry he could eat.

00:01:42.553 --> 00:01:45.533
Right. "Look how spicy my curries are."

00:01:45.533 --> 00:01:47.453
Yeah, that's quite funny.

00:01:47.453 --> 00:01:50.673
But he did drink a lot of water, which is not the best thing.

00:01:50.673 --> 00:01:52.053
What was I like as a baby?

00:01:52.053 --> 00:01:54.613
Uh-oh. You were so cute as a baby.

00:01:54.613 --> 00:01:58.743
Was I? Yeah, you were. You were very, very cute.

00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:05.473
You were like a little cherub, and you had blonde curly hair.

00:02:05.473 --> 00:02:12.653
And you were a lovely baby, but you had your own language,

00:02:12.653 --> 00:02:16.133
which I think is why you are good at languages now.

00:02:16.133 --> 00:02:18.873
Because you made up your own language, right,

00:02:18.873 --> 00:02:21.893
and only I could understand what you were saying.

00:02:21.893 --> 00:02:24.913
It's quite nice really, isn't it? Yeah, it was.

00:02:24.913 --> 00:02:28.203
What was your earliest childhood memory?

00:02:29.633 --> 00:02:32.743
I don't know what mine is, so don't worry if you don't know.

00:02:32.973 --> 00:02:39.993
I think just playing outside in the street with my friends and just running about.

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:45.093
And there was less traffic, and it felt safer back in the day.

00:02:45.093 --> 00:02:47.713
Did it? Yeah. Aww.

00:02:47.713 --> 00:02:51.993
Who was your best friend when you were young?

00:02:51.993 --> 00:02:56.333
My best friend at school was Anne Harper,

00:02:56.333 --> 00:03:02.073
and we were best friends right all the way through primary school.

00:03:02.073 --> 00:03:06.587
And she was a twin, and she had a brother.

00:03:06.587 --> 00:03:09.027
What happened? Did you lose touch?

00:03:09.027 --> 00:03:13.207
At 11, I passed my 11-plus. What was that?

00:03:13.207 --> 00:03:15.247
It was an exam that we used to have.

00:03:15.247 --> 00:03:18.947
We still have it technically in some places.

00:03:18.947 --> 00:03:25.267
I passed my 11-plus, much to my surprise, and I went to grammar school.

00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:29.507
So she went to a different school to me, and we lost touch.

00:03:29.507 --> 00:03:30.977
That's sad.

00:03:32.207 --> 00:03:36.307
Here's another question I've never asked you, because, you know,

00:03:36.307 --> 00:03:41.427
like, growing up as a child, I'm in my own world.

00:03:41.427 --> 00:03:42.827
As an adult, we're all busy.

00:03:42.827 --> 00:03:44.387
And you're still in your own world.

00:03:44.387 --> 00:03:47.267
And I'm still in my own world. But it is.

00:03:47.267 --> 00:03:49.727
What was your favourite meal as a child?

00:03:49.727 --> 00:03:52.427
Oh right, I can tell you this one.

00:03:52.427 --> 00:03:59.777
My favourite meal was fish fingers, peas, frozen peas, and mashed potatoes.

00:04:00.167 --> 00:04:01.847
So nothing much has changed, really? No.

00:04:01.847 --> 00:04:04.387
But I used to like to make a volcano

00:04:04.387 --> 00:04:08.357
of my mashed potatoes and put my peas in it.

00:04:09.227 --> 00:04:12.097
And I used to have that most Saturdays.

00:04:12.187 --> 00:04:14.637
Do you think you had a nice childhood?

00:04:14.727 --> 00:04:18.007
I did, until I was 11. Why?

00:04:18.007 --> 00:04:22.477
Well, like I said, we were able to play in the street. We ran free.

00:04:22.527 --> 00:04:24.477
Was it so bad, this new school?

00:04:24.967 --> 00:04:31.527
It was just so different to what I was used to.

00:04:31.527 --> 00:04:35.627
I was at an inner-city school that, you know, wasn't remarkable.

00:04:35.627 --> 00:04:36.940
So you were in quite a rough school,

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:40.940
and then suddenly in a very posh school. Yeah.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:43.860
What did it feel like being the, you know,

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:47.940
relatively poor kid, right, in a posh school?

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:54.060
Like, I'm sure you had issues with, like, buying the right uniform and shoes and stuff.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:59.740
Oh, definitely. Yeah, actually, I did feel like the poor kid in the posh school.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:05.710
There were, you know, people whose parents were doctors and played violin.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:11.080
But I didn't know that way of life existed until I went to that school,

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:14.400
because at my junior school everybody was the same, really.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.180
But I think the impact it has had is that I like to

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:25.280
have the correct clothing and shoes and equipment for anything that I'm doing.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.890
Do you wish you hadn't gone to that school? Oh, no.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:31.400
No, because it gave me a really good education, you know.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:36.620
So what did you enjoy most about school? That's what I want to know.

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:40.580
Oh, sport mostly. Sport? You're really sporty?

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:47.140
I was really sporty. Oh, right. Interesting. I didn't know you were that sporty. I was.

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:55.060
I ran, I did long jump, I played hockey, I played netball.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:06:00.860
And ironically, when we played tennis, me and my friends, we didn't like tennis.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.020
We used to just knock the ball out of court

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:06.490
and spend most of the lesson looking for it.
